From: VMForMac <alican.kuklaci@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] file-posix: treat ENXIO from SEEK_DATA on a compressed macOS file as "no info"

On macOS, lseek(SEEK_DATA) and lseek(SEEK_HOLE) fail with ENXIO at every
offset of an HFS+/APFS-compressed (decmpfs, UF_COMPRESSED) file.
find_allocation() reads ENXIO as "trailing hole", so block status reports
the whole file as zeros. Apple's installer compresses the files of an app
bundle, so the edk2 pflash firmware in Contents/Resources read back as
zeros and the guest never started. For a compressed file, report that we
know nothing (the caller then treats the range as data). Real sparse files
are not compressed and keep the normal hole detection.

---
--- a/block/file-posix.c
+++ b/block/file-posix.c
@@ -3185,7 +3185,24 @@
      */
     offs = lseek(s->fd, start, SEEK_DATA);
     if (offs < 0) {
-        return -errno;          /* D3 or D4 */
+        int err = errno;
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+        /*
+         * VMForMac: on macOS, SEEK_DATA and SEEK_HOLE fail with ENXIO at
+         * every offset of an HFS+/APFS-compressed (decmpfs, UF_COMPRESSED)
+         * file, even though the whole file is data.  Apple's installer
+         * compresses files in app bundles, so read-only firmware and ISOs
+         * in Contents/Resources would otherwise read back as all zeros.
+         * ENXIO is only a trailing hole for an uncompressed file; for a
+         * compressed one we learned nothing (D4), so report data.
+         */
+        struct stat st;
+        if (err == ENXIO && fstat(s->fd, &st) == 0 &&
+            (st.st_flags & UF_COMPRESSED)) {
+            return -ENOTSUP;
+        }
+#endif
+        return -err;            /* D3 or D4 */
     }
 
     if (offs < start) {
